Subject: Nootropics

Quoth Tom Morrow, verily I saith unto thee:
> 
> I find that my sources on nootropics do not discuss a few compounds that,
> apparently, those who package and sell nootropics consider useful.  I would
> appreciate information on the following (putative) nootropics:
> 
> Phosphatidylserine (found in the Life Extension Foundation's Cognitex);
> 
> Phosphatidylethanolamine (found in the LEF's Lecithin granules mix);
> 
> and the obviously related compunds,
> 
> Inositol (found in the LEF's Life Extension Mix); and 
> Phosphatidylinositol (found in the LEF's Lecithin granules mix).

This is all just junk from lecithin, which contains a variety of phosphatides.
What you basically have is inositol, serine, and ethanolamine.  Inositol is
quite useful, as it is a neurotransmitter like choline (and is often packaged
with choline; the ch. supplement I take is 50/50 ch. and ino.), and has other
beneficial effects.  Serine I have read something on, I might be able to find
that again.  Ethanolamine I dunno.  Rings a bell, but a distant one.

Don't go paying extra for this stuff, it is all in lecithin.  Personally I
recommend not buying from specialty outfits like LEF.  It is a rip off. Even
TwinLab, for all it's quality is far too high priced.  Keep in mind that vites
are parity products; "best" means "equal to".  When you pay 50% more for a 
12% "better" product, this is not equitable.  Just take more of the "lesser"
brand.  If this reasoning seems suspect to you, there is a detailed explantion
of the niceties in Pearson and Shaw's _Life_Extension_Companion_, in the section
about "natural" <guffaw!> vs. "artificial" vitamin E.  

Anyway, I recommend GNC's "Triple Lecithin".  Despite the name, and labelling
claims not withstanding, it is actually about double the "potency" (choline
content) of typical healthfood store lecithin.  TwinLab's expensive granules
are not much more potent, and the price ratio is ridiculous.  The GNC triple
lec. comes in capsule form, and thus is not so prone to rancidity (eating
rancid lecithin is worse than eating none, by orders of magnitude, since is
is chock full of oxidants).  GNC is a national chain, and is typically found
in malls.  Quality is medium to high, and prices are low to start with, plus
if you buy 1 you get a second bottle for 50% off.  Great deal.

I am in no way connected with GNC, and I am not a biologist, physician or
dietician, thus this is purely my opinion, and is not to be taken as medical
advice.
