Date: Mon, 24 May 1993 18:41:08 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul? 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu says:
> Perry replies to my entry in the "Extropianly Incorrect Topic of the Week
> contest."
> 
> >I think the hole in your argument, David, is a confusion of
> >engineering with science.
> >
> >Leonardo Da Vinci first hypothesized that flying was possible in a
> >serious way hundreds of years ago, and men unsuccessfully built flying
> >machines for centuries before the Wright Brothers did it "right".
> >
> >Babbage hypothesized that he could build a computer almost a century
> >early -- his experiment failed, but eventually we succeeded.
> >
> >The mere fact that something can be built in theory doesn't mean that
> >we yet have complete knowledge needed to perform the activity.
> 
> That would be a legitimate reply if I had claimed that the failure of AI to
> live up to its promises was a proof of the existence of the soul, but my
> claim was only that it was evidence.

I'm not sure it actually is evidence qua evidence. Its certainly
indicative of SOMETHING, but what is it indicative of? I'd claim that
we are dealing with several interlocking issues, and its very hard to
tease them apart. These issues include whether the mind is seated in
the brain, whether brain is a mechanistic device, whether we can
simulate that device with current or future technology, etc. I'd say
that the evidence is hardly clear given that no controlled experiment
has been done.

> Surely you would agree that if Babbage
> or Leonardo had succeeded, that would be evidence (very strong
> evidence--even proof if "succeed" means "we are certain he did it") in
> favor of the claim that what they suggested could be done.

Of course. However, absense of evidence is not evidence of absense.
The mere fact that no one had succeeded until 1904 in building a
flying machine did not make for good evidence it could not be done. It
failed to prove that it could be done, certainly, but when studying
non-controlled experiments, one must be very very careful about what
conclusions one can draw.

> Perry goes on to give other reasons for believing that the soul is, in his
> elegant term, "an epiphenomenon of a mechanistic system." My point was not
> that there was no evidence for that proposition--merely that the
> performance of AI so far is evidence against.

I will agree that the performance of AI thus far fails to give
positive evidence for my position -- however, it fails to give
negative evidence, and studies of the brain itself tend to give
positive evidence. Given that external evidence...

> And his evidence is only
> evidence, not proof--most people who believe in the existence of a soul in
> any of the usual senses of the term also believe that there is some
> connection between the soul and the brain, which is basically what the
> experiments he cites demonstrate.

Well, the problem with the notion of a hypothetical extranatural soul
somehow linked in with the brain is that we can't see it in operation.
I can observe any neuron in the brain that I choose to instrument, and
they all behave exactly as they always do : as if they are being
influenced by the standard laws of physics. For an extranatural soul
soul-brain connection to be in operation, one would expect to observe
neural behavior that could not be explained just from the behavior of
neurons -- certain extranatural neural events would have to be seen.
We have not, however, observed such phenomena, and not for a lack of
study of neurons in vivo.

I am not in any way suggesting that the case for strong AI is proven
-- I merely note that the case for strong AI seems much stronger than
the case for an extranatural soul.

Perry

