Date: Mon, 24 May 1993 02:15:56 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Chaitin & stochastic automata

Since axioms are random, :-), I'll liberally offer some
ideas of my own that have been inspired by this thread, with
no special claim to rigor.  I'll make up some of my own definitions,
and hopefully explain them sufficiently so that I don't step on
theoretical toes.  I'm doing this for fun, so if I'm all wet don't
take it too seriously!  If I'm partly wet feel free to point out
the holes in my shoes. 

I see that Pi can be expanded, but can it be compressed?  No
Turing Machine, given an infinite precision real that starts
with 3.14.... as input, can say "Aha!  That's exactly Pi!".  A TM does
exist that can determine that the number is closer to the Pi generator than 
it is to any other concise generator (see below). We can also define a TM 
that can say "that's Pi out to M decimal places" for any M: generate Pi to 
M places and compare.  But there will always exist a real number starting 
at digit M+1, for which we do not know whether it is more of Pi, or even 
if it is random or computable.

Challenge: prove that Pi is not Omega with its digits reversed. :-)
Intuitively it seems that we should be able to demonstrate the high 
improbability of this hypothesis, but I suspect it cannot be absolutely 
disproven within the Cantor/Turing/Chaitin framework of computability 
and real numbers.  

The term of art "computable" is a major source of confusion
here.  A random sequence is called "uncomputable", even though such a
sequence can be readily generated (dice, nuclear decay, etc.).  Pi
is called "computable" even though it has never been completely 
expanded.  This is because given an assumption of infinite time, 
a TM could in principle compute Pi, but it couldn't generate a
fully random sequence.  This suggests that TMs are not a complete 
model for "computing" machines, "thinking", "problem solving", whatever 
you want to call it.  More on that below.

The Pi acceptor TM seems to require a Pi generator built in; ie
the definition of Pi.  We define Pi by its compressed form, not by its 
expanded form, which we do not fully know.  It makes more sense to me 
to call Pi "expandable" rather than "compressible".  This makes even
more sense for the Mandelbrot set; given an expanded fractal it is often
notoriously difficult to find the original concise function which generated 
it.  One-way functions are expandable but not compressible.

Omega also has a concise definition, "the proportion of Turing
machines that will halt".  But we cannot use this definition to generate 
precise values of Omega.  We can expand it stochastically, eg by generating
and testing random TMs, as Chaitin has done.  Given infinite time, and an
infinite sequence of random numbers used to generate an infinite
number of TMs, we can expand Omega to an arbitrarily high precision.
We can also expand Pi with the Monte Carlo method (superimpose a circle 
on a square and throw uniformly distributed darts), with no upper bound 
on our confidence of the Mth digit.  Pi is stochastically expandable to 
arbitrary precision, as well as deterministically expandable to arbitrary 
precision (computable).  

Earlier I claimed that a Pi acceptor TM must have the definition
of Pi built in.  However, since Pi can be defined concisely in
terms of more primitive functions, arithmetic and iteration, we
can use genetic programming/symbolic regression to "reverse 
engineer" the precise definition of Pi, given the digits of Pi
to a large but finite sequence.   This is compression without
an _a priori_ compression of Pi embedded in the TM.  While
GP is itself Turing-equivalent (but see below), there is no guarantee 
that GP will find the correct solution.   The degree of confidence in 
our solution is probably bounded only by 100% for this problem (warning: 
theoretical GP work along these lines is lacking).  Assuming this
is so, I call Pi "stochastically compressible". 

Since we cannot induct Pi's definition via deterministic methods 
without that definition already built-in, and even then we cannot 
induct Pi to full precision, Pi is uncompressible via deterministic 
methods, as is Omega.

Is Omega stochastically compressible?  That is, if we know Omega
out to M digits with approaching 100% confidence, can we reverse
engineers something like Chaitin's definition using GP?  In other words,
can we rephrase the "proportion of TMs that will halt"
in terms of primitive functions that having nothing
to do _a priori_ with Omega's definition, but can be used to
construct it?   I'll leave this interesting problem for a
future post.

A random coin toss can be generated only in the sense that we
can generate from certain distributions (uniform, normal, Poisson, etc.).
We can also induct distributions, but not simplified formulas that
give the same sequence.  I suspect we can induct pseudorandom generators
given the old infinite resources & input data assumption; ditto for 
reverse engineering fractals (cf. caveat on GP theory).

Question to ponder: what is the smallest set of terminal functions
can be used to compose the definitions of all stochastically compressible 
numbers (including Omega if it belongs in that set)?

Here's a summary of my comparison between stochastic and
deterministic computation:

Deterministically expandable: Any finite digit M can be
generated by a TM with absolute confidence.  (This is
also the mathematical definition of a "computable" number).

Deterministically compressible: Smallest definition can be
inducted from finite sequence with absolute confidence.

Stochastically expandable: Any finite digit M can be 
generated stochastically, with confidence in the the
correctness of M approaching 100% as computation time
approaches infinity.

Stochastically compressible: Finite definition can be
stochastically inducted from finite sequence with confidence 
approaching 100% as sequence and computation time approach infinity.


		d. expand. d. compress.    s. expand.  s. compress.
Pi		yes  	   no		   yes		yes
Omega		no	   no		   *		**
Mandelbrot	yes	   hard		   yes	        hard?
Random sequence no         no		   (distribution only)

* iff we can approach 100% confidence at the limit, for any digit M
** iff Omega is stochastically expandable, and its definition
is of finite length and composed from finite alphabet


To model stochastic computation, I propose a "stochastic automata",
which consists of a Turing machine plus an input tape containing
an infinite random sequence.  I am most interested in those stochastic 
automata that halt with confidence approaching 100% as input tape and 
computation time approach infinity.  The perspective given here suggests 
that stochastic automata, or "satisficers", may be computationally more 
general than Turing machines, albeit not in the traditional arena of 
deterministic computable functions.  In other words, stochastic automata 
may provide concise specifications for machines that solve problems 
where Turing machines blow up, eg the halting problem for large Turing 
machines.

Note how my definition of the stochastic automaton is different from
the computer science definition of nondeterministic TM.  NDTM are 
deemed no more capable than DTM because any NDTM that halts at a 
certain point on the input tape can be converted to a DTM that will 
also halt at the same exact point.  The halting of a stochastic
automaton cannot be precisely predicted, nor duplicated, but it
can be estimated probabilistically.

A critic at this point might object to the infinite tape of
random numbers as violating the "ground rule" of the Church-Turing
thesis, which says that any "computation" must do "finite labor at
each step".  To this I respond (1) the TM model also assumes infinite 
tape(s), and (2) random numbers are just as abundant in nature as 
zero-entropy blank tapes.

Important to note: Any *finite* computation, which all real-world
computations are, can actually be modeled by the weaker finite
state machine instead of a TM, albeit often with great loss of conciseness. 

Example: the language a(N)b(N) ("N" printed N times followed by
"b" printed N times) cannot be recognized by a finite machine if
the maximum N expected is not know beforehand, but if N is known we can 
construct a machine with O(N) states to solve the problem.  An O(c)
finite machine with an infinite stack can also solve the problem.
Since, in the real world, there are no infinite stacks, the "power"
of this model comes from the O(N) compression of the machine's
definition, not from its potential to parse infinite strings.

The Turing machine is the most concise way to describe the most
general class of traditional computations, but as we have seen 
problems can arise when TMs try to answer questions about 
other TMs, for example whether an arbitrary TM will halt.

Similarly, any finite stochastic automata can be modeled by a Turing
machine, also losing concisenss, or by a finite automata, losing even 
more conciseness.  As a trivial example, pseudorandom number generators 
provide suboptimal entropy and finite precision, and with some effort 
can be formally modeled as TMs.  Stochastic automata simplify the matter 
by assuming true random number generators of infinite precision.   I
suspect that many stochastic simulations, genetic programming, etc. can 
also be modeled much more concisely by stochastic automata.

Specifically, Chaitin's program can probably be modeled more concisely 
by a stochastic automaton.  It expands Omega by generating legions of 
psuedorandom TMs, converging on the probability of a random TM halting, 
which fortuitously corresponds to the digits of Omega.   His
Omega-generating code is infinitely shorter than Omega, just
as a Pi generator is infinitely shorter than Pi. The finding that
O(N) programs yield < N digits of Omega only means that > O(N)
random digits are needed to generate N digits of Omega, not that
Chaitin needs to keep expanding his code by hand every time he
wants to calculate new digits.  Stochastic automata give us a new, 
broader definition of "computable" that corresponds to what Chaitin 
meant when he said, "in the limit, Omega becomes a computable sequence".

To repeat the caveat, much of this is my own initial hypothesizing;
none of the stochastic stuff has been formally proven.  If there is 
sufficient interest I will work on formalizing these hypotheses.

Nick Szabo					szabo@techbook.com

