Date: Mon, 24 May 93 16:02:49 PDT
From: mwm@wwtc.timeplex.com (mark m 623)
Subject: Ki

  Yes, there are good logical explanations for the phenomenon called ki.
I know, I spent a long time finding them to explain what I was seeing.
The explanations helped me relax around the subject, and that freed me to
learn how to manipulate it. I'm a Westerner and a very technical guy, I've 
been an agnostic since grade school and I don't believe half of what I see,
and *nothing* that I hear.
  So I dutifully brought endorphins and bio-feedback, and alpha states into
the ki model. I looked into the dynamics of breaking bricks with the bare
hand, spotted the carney tricks, discarded them, and was still left with
enough cement to break the knuckles if only mechanical force was involved.
  Well, after giving the left side of my brain something to chew on, it was
content to let the right side play, assured that no mystical, magickal,
irrational concepts were going to undermine my model of Reality.
  Actually, my techy background enhanced my understanding at times. All the
other students looked at the I Ching hexagrams and saw jibberish. I looked 
sideways and saw 0-63 in binary. 
  Being the demonstrator (i.e.; "dummy") for the class gave me a good 
oppurtunity to develope some manifestations of ki. I had already learned
how to make pain go away (excuse me, my body learned how to make pain go
away, I still don't nknow how I do it, I'm just glad I do). Actually, anyone
can learn it. Just get some big guy to hit you and kick you a lot. Maybe jab
you hard in various pressure points. Extreme joint locks and being thrown
forcefully to the floor are also recommended. Somewhere along the line your
body will decide that pain isn't fun and learn to deal with it. Or possibly,
my brain got tired of the distraction and learned how to interpret it 
differently. I lean toward the latter, because I never felt numb. I was always
aware of the fact that "This hurts" but it wasn't (here's where English
starts letting me down) important. Somewhere along the line, my brain (the
chemical/machine part, not *me*) decided that pain wasn't very important, but
that damage *is*.
  About 12 months into training (this is attending classes 5 nights a week, 
private lessons Monday through Friday, tournaments on weekends) i stopped 
bruising. I know, I know, I can't expect anyone to accept that on faith.
But this is what I observed:
  If I bumped my shin in the dark or some such, I'd bruise.
  If I saw a punch/kick/stick/shinai coming I wouldn't.
  This protection did not extend to my face.

I also "learned" how to do some of the ki "tricks". The unbendable arm, the
immovable stance. I say "learned" because all I did was relax, assured that i could do it, and did it. Visualization seemed to be the major component.
  
 And this is what made Zen palatable:
  All the riddles, the non-sequiters, the parables of Zen are designed
to keep the left side of your brain busy, so the right side can get some
quality time. And when you keep in mind the fact that during orgasm *all*
brain activity switched to the right side, and in an Alpha state, *all* 
cancel that last......

In a total Alpha state, all activity is on the right side.

  When dealing with this kind of subject, I find most rational logical
explanations to be about as helpful as "mass hynosis". A comforting
phrase, but what the hell does it mean?



Mark W. McFadden

