Harry, shifting the topic back to david's point about probabilities,
writes:

I think that given that Da Vinci couldn't build a flying machine 
did give evidence that it was difficult to build or impossible to build
a flying machine at that point in time.

Likewise, from Babbage, who was able to demonstrate key core aspects
of his technology and win contracts from the British government to
build at least one of his machines, we could predict that it was very
difficult to build such machines. Given the effort he made it seems
(post hoc?) that it would be difficult to build such a machine for
quite a while (it would take until 1992 in fact).

We don't have the perfect hindsite that we now have in the previous
examples. I think there is common ground here between Perry and DDFR.

I read David as really saying the evidence is quite strong that we
will not be able to build AI's (note the "we," meaning us at this
time, in the next few years). 

I read Perry's as saying no matter how bad it looks there is no reason
to think it can't be done in the future.

Like Babbage and Da Vinci we may have to weight until not only are new
tools invented but whole new mediums as well.

