HEADLINE: Scrambler Contract Award Spurs Protest

BYLINE: By NEIL MUNRO; Defense News Staff Writer

DATELINE: WASHINGTON

BODY:

In a move expected to greatly expand the market for voice-scrambling
technology, the U.S. government is awarding a sole-source contract to
AT&T for procurement of a Pentagon-developed voice-scrambling device.

The pending award has sparked protests from defense electronics
companies. The Hall, a marketing official for Motorola Inc., based in
Schaumberg, Ill., said May 4 that "Motorola does not have a similar
agreement with the government."

"We're pretty unhappy about it," Stephen Bryen, president of Secured
Communications Technologies Inc., Silver Spring, Md., said May 5.
Bryen's firm has developed a similar system.  "We were never given an
opportunity to participate."

Dubbed the Clipper Chip, the new device can be installed in a compact
electronics box.  The box, which can be easily attached to a
telephone, then automatically scrambles conversations defeating
eavesdroppers, such as spies seeking industrial secrets.

The Clipper Chip was announced April 16 by White House officials.
That same day, New York-based AT&T announced that it was including the
government-developed Clipper Chip in all of its voice scrambling
products.

The Defense Department's National Security Agency (NSA), based in Fort
Meade, Va., developed the Clipper chip at the request of officials at
the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) in
Gaithersburg, Md.

Judy Emmel, an NSA spokeswoman, declined comment May 3, referring
questions to NIST officials.  NSA is responsible for intercepting
other countries' military signals and protecting U.S.  signals from
interception.

"AT&T has been very active in developing new [telephone security]
technologies . . . and the government believed that it needed to
quickly buy large numbers" of Clipper Chip voice-scrambling devices,
NIST spokesman Mat Heyman said May 4.

Among the security devices AT&T has marketed is the STU-III voice
scrambling phone, of which there are more than 200,000 currently in
use by combat personnel and defense industry officials.

In late 1992, government officials approached AT&T officials and told
them about the Clipper Chip because the telephone giant was planning
to markets its own scrambling device, the Telephone Security Device
3600, Lynn McNulty, NIST communications security expert, said May 6.

The new government technology has the potential to create a vast
market as defense businesses, commercial companies and even private
citizens make increased use of the standardized Clipper Chip voice-
scrambling devices, AT&T spokesman David Ameke said May 6.  The market
has been hindered by the variety of incompatible voice- scrambling
devices produced by many large and small companies, Arneke said.

According to a Motorola official, the use by the government of AT&T
Clipper

Chip devices boosts' AT&Ts chances of selling its products throughout
the federal government.  Because the AT&T device uses specialized
telephone technology that is different from technology used by other
telephone companies, government officials will find it easier to buy
additional AT&T telephones than undergo the trouble of ensuring that
Clipper Chip -telephones are compatible with competing companies, he
said.

But McNulty said the government wants to widely distribute the Clipper
Chip technology, allowing Clipper Chip devices to easily work with
other manufacturers' products.

"The government intends to create a level playing field . . . [but]
there are a lot of details to be worked out," he said.



