Date: Mon, 24 May 93 22:48:51 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Chaitin & stochastic automata

Nick Szabo offers some fun thoughts:

> I see that Pi can be expanded, but can it be compressed?

You mean recognized?  What would it mean for a machine to recognize
"Pi"?  The only thing I can imagine is that a number matches
the output of some program (formula) labeled "Pi".  So I'd think
that something like the following could work:  given an n bit string
that is "matched" by a k-bit program (k < n), we know that the probability
of there being such a k-bit program is about 2^(k-n).  This could be used
as a probability that the string isn't Pi.  You're right, though --
we can't be sure.

> Challenge: prove that Pi is not Omega with its digits reversed. :-)

Well, not to be a wet blanket, but does this make sense?  If you
mean prove that Omega cannot be produced by some (more or less)
simple transformation of the digits of Pi, then I could do that...

More questions:

 * Only compressible numbers have finite expansions, right?

 * Why is the Mandelbrot set deterministically compressible
   when Pi isn't?  The M set has an infinite number of points;
   can we ever be SURE it's really the M set?
 
 * Would you say that numbers that are stochastically expandable
   are also "exhaustively" expandable?  E.g. instead of throwing
   random darts to estimate Pi, can't I estimate it by deterministically
   placing darts at finer and finer grains?  Instead of randomly
   approximating Omega, can't I deterministically run TMs in parallel
   to achieve the same effect?

[on using GP to stochastically compress Pi]
> The degree of confidence in 
> our solution is probably bounded only by 100% for this problem (warning: 
> theoretical GP work along these lines is lacking).

Well, provided that the function and terminal sets are rich enough,
given infinite time the solution is bound to be found.  Is this
what you mean?

> Since we cannot induct Pi's definition via deterministic methods 
> without that definition already built-in, and even then we cannot 
> induct Pi to full precision, Pi is uncompressible via deterministic 
> methods

But couldn't an "exhaustive" search of the same space that GP
stochastically searches eventually find the same solution?

> To model stochastic computation, I propose a "stochastic automata",

This is a cool idea (and note that it's a *generalization* of the normal
TM, so shares all of its positive capabilities).  The question is whether
it has any fewer limitations.  Does anybody know if it's been proposed
before? I'd bet it has, but you never know... for now I'll call it an
S-machine (for Szabo).

> The halting of a stochastic
> automaton cannot be precisely predicted, nor duplicated, but it
> can be estimated probabilistically.

I see what you're getting at -- but what if the S-machine doesn't
look at its random input?  What if we can't tell whether it will
look at the random input?  Aren't we back to the normal halting
problem?

> Similarly, any finite stochastic automata can be modeled by a Turing
> machine, also losing conciseness

Oh no!  I don't think you want to say that!  Otherwise, all we've
done is build a little "generate pseudo-random number" subroutine
into a normal TM, which isn't very interesting...  the savings in
conciseness of doing this are pretty small... the interesting issue
is whether we gain anything fundamental with S-machines.

That's a question that deserves some thought.  Interesting stuff.

derek            A.S. member 3/15


