Date: Mon, 24 May 93 21:16:47 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

Homesteading has advantages outside the narrow economic framework.

An auction requires an auctioneer, and a seller.  Who is selling the
universe?  All mankind?  If so, how do we divvy up the proceeds?  These
problems are avoided by homesteading.

Also, homesteading is arguably the natural way ownership is created,
as those who take possession of property develop it and defend it.  Telling
someone who's spent a decade making an asteroid into a home that he has
to give it up because it's been auctioned off back on earth may not work.

Auctions seem to require a police force which is patrolling the universe
to enforce the deeds to land.  If such a police force existed, those who
control it may not be so willing to let the land go, as we see today in
a world in which much of the land is owned by states.

The issue of the proper units of ownership (orbits, asteroids, steradians,
whatever) is hard.  I suspect that we won't know the answer until people
live there, and by then it's too late for the auction.  Once people are
out there, if they decide that they need "sunlight rights" or "periastron
slots" or whatever else it turns out is natural, they can create these
with court systems much as we create rights to quiet, unobstructed views,
or clean air today.

This may not be economically perfectly optimal, but I think it represents
a realistic compromise between human nature and mathematical perfection.

(Of course, as we discussed last year, the existance of fast replication
technology, nanotech or otherwise, presents problems for the homesteading
model by allowing near-monopolies to be easily formed.)

Hal

